    @{
        Slug = 'wasp43b'
        Title = 'WASP-43 b Exoplanet Transit'
        DateLabel = '30 April 2020'
        DateIso = '2020-04-30'
        CategoryKey = 'observations'
        CategoryLabel = 'Observations'
        Thumb = 'wasp43b1k.jpg'
        Image = 'wasp43b.jpg'
        Alt = 'Photometric light curve showing the transit of exoplanet WASP-43 b'
        Summary = 'A backyard detection of an exoplanet transit using differential photometry.'
        Body = @"
<p>WASP-43 b is about the same diameter as Jupiter and orbits extremely close to its star, completing a revolution every 19 hours. From Earth, its path sometimes crosses the face of the magnitude 12.4 star and blocks about two percent of its light.</p>
<p>I observed a predicted transit on 30 April 2020 with my five-inch refractor from suburban Auckland, New Zealand.</p>
<p>The observation used differential photometry: repeated images of the target were compared with reference stars to reveal a very small change in brightness.</p>
<p>I captured 417 images of 17 seconds each. Conditions began well but deteriorated over the three-hour session as high cloud moved through; nine frames were rejected. The remaining images were calibrated and registered in PixInsight, then measured in AstroImageJ.</p>
<p>The resulting light curve clearly shows the expected 0.025-magnitude dip. It is remarkable that amateur equipment can detect a planet orbiting another star.</p>
"@
    }
